%description:

RFC 6298 -- each expiry of the retransmission timer doubles the timeout.

Checks RFC6298-BACK-1 (must) and RFC6298-EARLY-1 (must).

RFC 6298 section 5.5: every time the timer expires, the timeout is doubled. Section 5: a
segment is never retransmitted sooner than one timeout after its previous transmission.

The relay discards every copy of host A's data segment, so the segment never arrives and the
timer expires again and again. The intervals between consecutive transmissions of the same
sequence number are the timeouts that were in force, one after another, and each must be
twice the one before it.

The check asserts the ratio and not the values. The initial timeout is the subject of
`Rfc6298InitialTimeout.test`; this check is about the doubling alone, so it holds on a model
whose first timeout is any value.

**One of the three expected observations cannot be made.** The check asks for the doubling
from both sides: from the link, as the interval, and from the estimator, as the timeout
variable after each expiry. The model publishes no signal on expiry. `TcpBaseAlg` emits
`rto` only from `receivedDataAck`, and the doubling happens in the expiry path
(src/inet/transportlayer/tcp/flavours/TcpBaseAlg.cc:213), which emits nothing. So only the
link side is checked here. The note in `checks.md` is right that keeping both would be
better: a model that doubled the variable but not the interval would pass one and fail the
other. Until the expiry publishes the value, this check cannot tell those two apart.

The doubling is bounded at 240 seconds by `MAX_REXMIT_TIMEOUT`, which RFC6298-MAX-1 permits.
The three intervals this check reads are far below that bound, so the cap does not enter.

**The model conforms.** The run doubles the timeout at every expiry, and says so itself:

    Performing retransmission #1; increasing RTO from 3s  to 6s, and cancelling RTT measurement
    Performing retransmission #2; increasing RTO from 6s  to 12s, and cancelling RTT measurement
    Performing retransmission #3; increasing RTO from 12s  to 24s, and cancelling RTT measurement

Those lines also show the model cancels the round-trip measurement on a retransmission,
which is Karn's rule and the subject of a check of its own.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

#include <cmath>

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// A pure acknowledgment carries the same port and no SYN, so every step here also requires
// a data-bearing segment.
static bool dataSegment(const MatchContext& context)
{
    return context.event.packet != nullptr && context.event.packet->getByteLength() > 100;
}

// The interval that ends at this event must be twice the interval between the two captured
// moments. One per cent of the earlier interval is far tighter than any other law would fit
// and far wider than the transmission times involved.
static bool intervalDoubled(const MatchContext& context, const char *earlier, const char *later)
{
    double a = context.get(earlier).doubleValue();
    double b = context.get(later).doubleValue();
    double previous = b - a;
    double current = context.event.time.dbl() - b;
    return previous > 0 && std::fabs(current - 2 * previous) <= 0.01 * previous;
}

static bool secondIntervalDoubled(const MatchContext& context) { return intervalDoubled(context, "t0", "t1"); }
static bool thirdIntervalDoubled(const MatchContext& context) { return intervalDoubled(context, "t1", "t2"); }

static cValue eventTime(const PacketEvent& event) { return cValue(event.time.dbl()); }

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc6298_backoff_doubling")
        // Discard every copy of the data segment. Only one segment is ever in flight,
        // because nothing is acknowledged, so this is every copy of the first one.
        .intercept(tap("relay")
                       .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                       .minBytes(100)
                       .drop().describe("every copy of host A's data segment"))
        // The original transmission.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment)
                  .capture("firstSeq", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  .capture("t0", eventTime)
                  .describe("host A's data segment, first transmission")
                  .within(2.0))
        // The first retransmission. Its interval is the initial timeout, whatever that is.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.sequenceNo == {firstSeq} && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment).first()
                  .capture("t1", eventTime)
                  .describe("the first retransmission")
                  .within(20.0))
        // The second retransmission, after an interval twice the first.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.sequenceNo == {firstSeq} && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment).first()
                  .assertEvent(secondIntervalDoubled)
                  .capture("t2", eventTime)
                  .describe("the second retransmission, twice as long after the first")
                  .within(40.0))
        // And the third, after an interval twice the second.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.sequenceNo == {firstSeq} && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment).first()
                  .assertEvent(thirdIntervalDoubled)
                  .describe("the third retransmission, twice as long after the second")
                  .within(80.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc6298_backoff_doubling compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;
import inet.protocoltest.PacketTap;

// Two hosts with a relay on the link -- note: NO tester (attached at runtime).
network BackoffNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        relay: PacketTap { @display("p=400,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> relay.a;
        relay.b     <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = BackoffNet
sim-time-limit = 60s
seed-set = 0

# static ARP: the only frames on the wire are the TCP/IP ones.
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 500B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = -1s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpSinkApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc6298_backoff_doubling: PASS
